{:href "/athletes/38467"} "Giuseppe Ramani"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/38509"} "Aldo Tarlao"] " (a 1948 Olympic silver medalist), and " [:a {:href "/athletes/38412"} "Luciano Marion"] " had won the last three European Championships in the coxed pairs and, in the absence of many other metrics, were considered the favorites to win the event at the 1952 Games. Their greatest challengers were the Swiss, whose trio of " [:a {:href "/athletes/39763"} "Walter Lüchinger"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/39810"} "Alex Siebenhaar"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/39762"} "Walter Ludin"] ", were runners-up in the last two European Championships. Other more distant threats were Belgium’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/35719"} "Hippolyte Mattelé"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/35706"} "Eugeen Jacobs"] ", part of the 1949 and 1950 European bronze medal-winning crews, who brought " [:a {:href "/athletes/35752"} "Kamiel Van Dooren"] " with them to Helsinki, and Denmark’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/36582"} "Svend Pedersen"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/36608"} "Poul Svendsen"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/36493"} "Jørgen Frantzen"] ", who represented a recent strong national tradition in this event."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Switzerland’s failure to advance beyond the opening round was a surprise, almost as much as the strength of France, Germany, and the United States who, along with Italy, were the winners of the four heats. The Americans and the Belgians were eliminated in the semi-finals, with France’s trio of " [:a {:href "/athletes/37091"} "Raymond Salles"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/37045"} "Gaston Mercier"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37029"} "Bernard Malivoire"] " posting the fastest time. They repeated this feat in the final, earning a gold medal and Malivoire the distinction, at age 14, of being the youngest gold medalist at the Games. Germany’s trio of " [:a {:href "/athletes/37919"} "Heinz Manchen"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/37838"} "Helmut Heinhold"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/37942"} "Helmut Noll"] " earned a surprise silver, while Denmark settled for bronze and Italy missed the podium entirely."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} "France"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "8"} [:a {:href "/athletes/37091"} "Raymond Salles"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37045"} "Gaston Mercier"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37029"} "Bernard Malivoir"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} "Unified Team of Germany"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "8"} [:a {:href "/athletes/37919"} "Heinz Manchen"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37838"} "Helmut Heinhold"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/37942"} "Helmut Noll"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} "Denmark"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/DEN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/DEN.png"}] "DEN"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "8"} [:a {:href "/athletes/36582"} "Svend Pedersen"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/36608"} "Poul Svendsen"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/36493"} "Jørgen Frantzen"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} "Italy"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olymp